# Bytes are identity in this repository: `tools/check_receipt.py` re-derives a
# SHA-256 over the files on disk and rejects a receipt when they moved.
#
# The committed fixtures under `vectors/` carry CRLF, because they were written
# on Windows and committed without normalisation. That is harmless while
# nobody re-encodes them, and fatal the moment somebody does:
# `git add --renormalize`, or a commit from an editor that rewrites line
# endings, moves every case digest and invalidates `receipts/*.json`.
#
# So the content-addressed parts are declared non-text: no EOL conversion at
# commit, at checkout, or on any platform. A clone hashes to the same bytes
# whatever the reader's Git defaults are.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Content-addressed: never normalise, never re-encode.
# `-text` alone is not enough: the `eol=lf` inherited from `*` above still
# triggers a checkout conversion. `-eol` unsets it, so the bytes on disk are
# the bytes in the blob, on every platform.
vectors/** -text -eol
receipts/** -text -eol
keys/** -text -eol

# The evidence lock is a one-byte binary marker.
*.lock binary
